-- title:
--   Theorem 2.4(b) — probability and oracle budget (corrected: zero-noise execution completed)
-- statement:
--   Let $f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ be differentiable and bounded below, with actual gradient $g$ that is $L$-Lipschitz, where $L>0$. Fix $x_1$, noise level $\sigma\ge0$, and arbitrary $\widetilde D>0$. The Borel oracle obeys conditional-mean A1 with unconditional squared error moment at most $\sigma^2$ along all calls. Let $f^*=\inf f$ and $D_f=\sqrt{2(f(x_1)-f^*)/L}$. For any $\epsilon>0$ and $0<\Lambda<1$, prescribe
--
--   $$S=\lceil\log_2(2/\Lambda)\rceil,$$
--   $$N=\left\lceil\max\left\{\frac{32L^2D_f^2}{\epsilon},\left[\frac{32L(\widetilde D+D_f^2/\widetilde D)\sigma}{\epsilon}\right]^2\right\}\right\rceil,\qquad T=\left\lceil\frac{24(S+1)\sigma^2}{\Lambda\epsilon}\right\rceil.$$
--
--   Execute the counted two-phase method: $S$ independent RSG candidates each using a uniform output index independent of its own noise; $T$ validation queries per candidate for positive noise; and least-index selection by minimum estimated-gradient norm. Within a run noise may depend on the past. Validation streams may share samples, provided each stream obeys A1 conditional on its known candidate and prior information. At zero noise use step $1/L$ and the stored replies at the candidates, making no validation calls. At $N=0$ return $x_1$ and the empty trace.
--
--   The returned point $\bar x^*$ is measurable, its success event is measurable, and both
--
--   $$\Pr\{\|g(\bar x^*)\|^2\le\epsilon\}\ge1-\Lambda,\qquad \forall\omega,\quad\operatorname{calls}(\operatorname{execution}(\omega))\le S(N+T)$$
--
--   hold. The cost is the length of the query trace generated by the actual recursion and validation loops.
--
--   This is the full-domain probability-and-cost guarantee in (2.24)–(2.27). The existing Proved positive-noise probability clause is included by reference; the new goal also contains its missing operational cost and the undefined-case completions. The paper gives explicit constants 32 and 24 here; its subsequent asymptotic summary (2.32) is not substituted for them.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Dimension is arbitrary, including zero. No convexity, attainment of $f^*$, light-tail assumption, finite noise support, conditional variance bound or independence of successive within-run samples is required.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: sigma=0 leaves (2.13) undefined and gives T=0 in the average (2.21) → use gamma=1/L and select by oracle replies stored at each candidate index, with zero validation calls; reason: the singleton exact quadratic f(x)=x^2/2, x1=1, L=Dt=epsilon=1, Lambda=1/2, sigma=0 has D_f=1, N=32, T=0 and satisfies all source assumptions; this completes only undefined operations without a true-gradient oracle
--   - correction: the prescribed N can be zero, leaving no distribution on 1,...,N → return x1 with an empty trace before creating any active run or index when N=0; reason: the exact zero objective with x1=0, L=Dt=epsilon=1, Lambda=1/2, sigma=0 gives N=T=0; the parameter equations force sigma=D_f=0 whenever N=0
--   - reading: A1(a) writes an expectation of an oracle at a random input → the conditional mean given past query information equals the actual gradient; reason: Eq. (2.10), p. 7 uses this conditional interpretation; the error second moment remains unconditional
--   - reading: log in (2.24) has no displayed base → log base two, with exactly S=ceil(log_2(2/Lambda)); reason: the proof on p. 13 requires 2^(-S) at most Lambda/2; natural log fails, for example at Lambda=1/10
--   - generalization: Borel noise and probability spaces with A1 along the source sequence → arbitrary measurable noise and probability spaces, with A1 required only through the executed finite horizon; reason: the Borel source model and its all-time A1 specialize to these laws; no source instance is lost
--   - encoding: the source counts S full runs and T validation queries per candidate in prose → each oracle call emits one record and calls is the trace length, bounded pointwise by S(N+T); reason: this formalizes (2.27) with unit multiplier, including separate evaluations when samples are recycled
--   - encoding: (2.21) allows any candidate of minimum estimated-gradient norm → scan candidates in increasing order and retain the least index among tied minima, with measurability proved in the conclusion; reason: this is a concrete permitted tie rule; neither measurability nor a selected-output performance bound is assumed
--   - encoding: one-based random-index RSG trajectory and implicit integrability → computed Euclidean trajectory and stored query lookup, with genuine integrability of every oracle return and squared error; reason: the finite-query interface expresses the same expectations and index distribution without using integral defaults
-- source:
--   Ghadimi and Lan, Stochastic First- and Zeroth-Order Methods for Nonconvex Stochastic Programming, arXiv:1309.5549v1, https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.5549v1, p. 12, Theorem 2.4(b), (2.24)–(2.27), proof p. 13; solution definition p. 10; full-run implementation p. 6; (2.21) p. 11

import Definitions.Def_GhadimiLanAudit_TwoPhase_Sampling

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace GhadimiLanAudit.TwoPhase

/-- Theorem 2.4(b), (2.24)-(2.27), arXiv:1309.5549v1, p. 12 (proof p. 13).
Readings: conditional A1 as in (2.10), log base two as required by 2^(-S)≤Λ/2.
Corrections only where execution is undefined: σ=0 uses γ=1/L and cached
oracle replies, without validation calls; N=0 returns x1 and the empty trace.
For the exact quadratic oracle with L=Dt=ε=1, Λ=1/2, σ=0, x1=1,
N=32 and T=0, so both printed divisions are undefined. The constant zero
objective gives N=T=0, hence no index on {1,...,N}. -/
theorem two_phase_oracle_complexity {n : ℕ} {Ξ Ω : Type*}
    [MeasurableSpace Ξ] [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (P : Problem n Ξ) (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    (ε Λ : ℝ) (hε : 0 < ε) (hΛ0 : 0 < Λ) (hΛ1 : Λ < 1)
    (A : Input P (runCount Λ) (iterationCount P ε) Ω)
    (hlaw : SamplingLaw (T := validationCount P ε Λ) P μ A) :
    let result := fun ω => twoPhaseExec (T := validationCount P ε Λ) P A ω
    Measurable (fun ω => (result ω).value) ∧
    MeasurableSet {ω | ‖P.g (result ω).value‖ ^ 2 ≤ ε} ∧
    ENNReal.ofReal (1 - Λ) ≤ μ {ω | ‖P.g (result ω).value‖ ^ 2 ≤ ε} ∧
    ∀ ω, calls (result ω) ≤ runCount Λ * (iterationCount P ε + validationCount P ε Λ) := by sorry

end GhadimiLanAudit.TwoPhase
